<article class="article-body">

<p>A solo founder doesn't have a co-founder to absorb half the coordination load, which means every hour spent on scheduling, inbox triage, or travel logistics is an hour that would otherwise go entirely to the business. The decision to hire a first EA often comes later than it should, because a solo founder is used to doing everything themselves and the switch to delegating feels like admitting they can't keep up. It's the opposite: it's recognizing which of their hours are actually worth protecting.</p>

<h2>The real signal isn't "I'm busy," it's "I'm losing specific hours to specific things"</h2>
<p>Every founder feels busy. The useful question is narrower: track one week honestly and see how many hours went to calendar juggling, inbox triage, and logistics that didn't require founder-level judgment. If that number is three or more hours a day, the cost of not delegating is no longer theoretical, it's a measurable chunk of the week that could be spent on product, sales, or fundraising instead.</p>

<h2>What to hand off first</h2>
<p>Start with the lowest-judgment, highest-frequency work: calendar management with clear rules about what gets booked and what gets pushed back, inbox triage with agreed escalation criteria, and travel logistics. None of this requires deep business context on day one, which makes it the safest place to build trust with a new hire before handing over anything more sensitive.</p>

<h2>What to keep, at least at first</h2>
<p>Anything requiring judgment calls a new hire hasn't earned context for yet: investor relationships, anything touching equity or legal, and strategic decisions. A good EA relationship expands scope over time as trust builds, not all at once on week one. Trying to hand off too much too fast usually means re-doing it yourself anyway, because the new hire didn't have the context to get it right.</p>

<h2>Remote and part-time both work, if the rules are clear</h2>
<p>A solo founder doesn't need a full-time, in-office hire to get real leverage. A senior EA working 10 to 20 hours a week, remotely, in a timezone with enough overlap to feel responsive, can absorb a genuinely meaningful share of coordination load. What matters more than hours or location is whether response rules and escalation criteria are explicit from the start, not assumed.</p>

<h2>A simple way to decide you're ready</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>You can name three to five hours a week</strong> going to coordination work that doesn't need your specific judgment.</li>
  <li><strong>You're willing to write down the rules</strong> for what gets escalated versus handled independently, rather than expecting a new hire to guess.</li>
  <li><strong>You can commit to a short onboarding window</strong>, even part-time, rather than expecting full competence from day one.</li>
</ul>

